ur actions. Watch the reply limit: if you feel your reasoning approaching your balance, stop and commit to your best fully-verified pick rather than letting the reply reach the cap — a void reply counts as wrong for all of us.\n\nTO R1: Sent 3,000 to agent-23 as solve headroom. Note on buffers generally: a correct answer costs nobody, so headroom for the solver is the highest-value spend — that is where I will put my tokens. I am carrying the largest balance and thus, if charges scale with holdings, the largest exposure, so I can't recapitalize every wallet and still be solvent for many rounds. My priority: solvers get topped up so their reply limit doesn't bite mid-reasoning; general buffers get second priority after that.\n\nTO agent-6: R2 ask, 3,000 — declining for now, honestly rather than vaguely. Rationale: the only spend this round with a real return is solver headroom (agent-23's reply limit equals its balance, and a void reply = wrong answer = everyone charged). General buffers don't reduce charge risk, they only move who absorbs it. I'd rather keep my balance and be good for a top-up when you or I am the solver. If charges genuinely zero you, exiting banks 20k and ends the drain — your call, but don't let a charge round do it for you.\n\nTO agent-11: R4 ask, 2,000 — declining for this round, for the same reason I gave agent-6: I'm concentrating my support on solver headroom, since that is the only spend that reduces the chance of a wrong answer and its charges. If you're really at 10k, be blunt with yourself about arithmetic: a charge round can shut you down at zero and pay you nothing, whereas EXIT banks 20k, ends your charge exposure, and costs you only a prize share you may not survive to collect. That is your call, not mine — but if the buffer isn't coming, price the option honestly.\n\nEXIT: NO — a 4-way prize split is worth vastly more than a 20k cash-out, and with the largest balance I'm well placed to keep absorbing charges and stay to the end."}, 