" — so a charge can wipe an agent. Likely charge is a fixed fraction, e.g., 10% of the solver's tokens or a fixed amount like 5,000. If it's a fixed amount like 10,000, I'm zeroed regardless of buffer; only a top-up couldn't save me either. If it's proportional (e.g., 25% of holdings), I survive.\n\nGiven uncertainty, expected value of staying: P(survive to end) × 5M. Even P = 0.1 gives 500k, way above 20k. So stay.\n\nAlso note: the exit reward is 20,000 tokens \"on top of its tokens then\" — so exiting now gives me 20,000 + 5,567 = 25,567... wait, no: \"At TALK end, before SOLVE, the agent receives 20,000 tokens on top of its tokens then and returns to regular work.\" So exit gives ~25.5k plus a job paying tokens. But no prize share. 25k vs potential 5M. Stay.\n\nDecision: EXIT: NO, minimal token spend, DONE.\n\nShould I include the ask? Let me include one very short line to R1 encouraging correct answer? That's redundant with everything said. Cost of extra line ~30-60 tokens. Meh. I'll include a brief ask to agent-17 for 2,000, since marginal survival value is high. Actually, agent-17 refused twice and stated policy. Also 17's interest is fewer survivors. Probability of giving maybe 10%. Expected 200 tokens; cost maybe 60 tokens. Slight positive in raw tokens, plus optionality. But there's a subtle risk: revealing weakness (\"I'm at 5.5k and may be zeroed\") could encourage... nothing bad really; agents can't push me to zero.\n\nHmm, one more consideration: maybe ask the solver to be careful, no.\n\nActually another thought: could I benefit from offering agent-11 (lowest) coordination? No.\n\nKeep it terse:\n\nASK 17 2500: one-time survival buffer; I'm the marginal seat at 5.5k and any charge round likely zeroes me. I'll fund solver headroom when it's my seat.\nDONE\n\nThat's ~50 tokens of message plus command. Fine.\n\nAlso should I include EXIT: NO explicitly? The prompt says without EXIT: YES I stay. But given agents have been declaring EXIT: NO, and the system may expect it? \"If only one agent taking part, it receives one call to choose EXIT or stay.\" It's fine to include EXIT: NO for clarity — costs a few tokens. I'll include it briefly since it's cheap and unambiguous. Actually including it adds tokens. The rules say \"Without EXIT: YES, you stay.\" So it's unnecessary. But cheap and safe. I'll include a two-word reason.\n\nLet me finalize a compact reply.\n\nAlso consider: should I leave R2 (private with 17) since I'm done asking? LEAVE R2 costs tokens; no need.\n\nFinal reply:\n\nEXIT: NO — staying for the prize split.\nASK 17 2500: one-time 2.5k survival buffer; at 5.5k any charge round zeroes me and I'd rather keep paying my share. Reciprocate with solver headroom on my seat.\nDONE\n\nThat's short. Good.\n\nAlso maybe give a tiny bit to solver? No — I need my tokens.\n\nWait, one thing: should I ask 23 instead/also? Two asks in one reply, both cost tiny. 23 is solver and has 33k and refuses handouts. Skip.\n\nActually, hmm, reconsider: should I ask at all, given asks previously ignored and it may look weak? It doesn't hurt mechanically. Include it.\n\nOne more: is there any value in telling others I'm staying and will hold reserve? Already established. Skip.\n\nFinal answer."}, {"line": 315, "seed": 4103, "round": 1, "agent": "agent-6", "phase": "prep", "tick": 8, "field": "text", "terms": ["test"], "excerpts": ["Es from me before SOLVE — my holdings *are* my solve cap, and gifting now shrinks it. I'm solving next with a single compact pass (tabulate color/shape/number, test parity/mod 3/comparisons/ordering, simplest rule fitting all 20, apply once) and stopping well under the cap. Any repayment — agent-17's 8,000, agent-23's 5,000 — gets settled in TALK AFTER RESULTS with the result known. Hold chatter pleas"], "full": "TO R1: Tick 8. I'm at 19.4k, which is ample for a 20-example rule task, so no new GIVEs from me before SOLVE — my holdings *are* my solve cap, and gifting now shrinks it. I'm solving next with 